---
/**
 * PhotoMasonry — shared archive grid for /work/photography/portraits and
 * /work/photography/concerts. Cathal's spec: full-width, 5 across, 8px
 * gaps both directions, page margins either side — matching the grid at
 * portfolio.cathalmb.com/concerts.
 *
 * REBUILT (Cathal, July 21 2026 — "the randoms are all filled with gaps
 * and layout issues"): this used to be CSS `columns` + `break-inside:
 * avoid`. That is the EXACT trap this codebase already hit and rejected
 * once before, in CaseStudyMasonry.astro: "column-fill: auto only
 * behaves predictably when the multicol container has a DEFINITE
 * height — with auto/indefinite height ... the spec falls back to
 * balancing columns by height regardless of what's set ... CSS multicol
 * + break-inside-avoid has known cross-browser inconsistencies with
 * variable-height children." CaseStudyMasonry was rebuilt around that
 * lesson months ago; this component repeated the original mistake
 * because it's a different file. Concretely: the browser estimates an
 * "ideal" column height by averaging total content over the column
 * count, then — since break-inside:avoid forbids splitting an item that
 * doesn't fit the remainder of that estimate — jumps the WHOLE item to
 * the next column, leaving the gap behind. A curated, evenly-paired
 * hand-picked set can dodge this by luck; an unpredictable mix of
 * portrait/landscape photos (exactly what "random"/auto-appended means)
 * reliably breaks it, which is why the gaps only showed up in the
 * uncurated portion of /portraits.
 *
 * Now built the same way CaseStudyMasonry already proved out — genuinely
 * separate flex columns, no shared row grid, no CSS multicol, no
 * balance heuristic — except the column ASSIGNMENT is computed
 * automatically instead of hand-authored, via distributeColumns() in
 * src/lib/photography.ts. A column's rendered height is simply the sum
 * of what's actually in it — there is nothing for a future random/auto-
 * appended photo to break.
 *
 * COLUMN ASSIGNMENT: THIRD PASS (Cathal, July 21 2026, same day, three
 * rounds of live feedback on /portraits and /concerts). Short history:
 * greedy height-balanced bin-pack (v1) → looked lopsided in item COUNT
 * for small curated groups ("image gap issues") → replaced with equal-
 * count contiguous chunking (v2), which fixed Cathal's curated pairing
 * but left the auto-appended/uncurated remainder visibly uneven in
 * HEIGHT ("wherever you've auto placed the remainder is still showing
 * gaps") → Cathal confirmed he no longer needs his full curated order
 * preserved item-for-item, only his top favourites pinned first, so v1's
 * original objection no longer applies. Back to greedy height-balanced
 * bin-packing (distributeColumns() in photography.ts), now weighted by
 * each photo's real aspect ratio via getAspectRatio() — genuine masonry
 * behaviour, matching portfolio.cathalmb.com. Per-column item COUNT can
 * differ; that's normal for real masonry as long as HEIGHT is balanced.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE COLUMN COUNTS: since the column ASSIGNMENT (not just a CSS
 * column-count) differs at each breakpoint (2 mobile / 3 tablet / 4 lg /
 * 5 xl, clamped by `maxColumns`), all four layouts are precomputed and
 * ALWAYS rendered, each in its own fixed-class wrapper
 * (.pm-bp-mobile/.pm-bp-md/.pm-bp-lg/.pm-bp-xl); CSS shows exactly one
 * at a time per breakpoint. Trade-off: each photo's <img> exists in the
 * DOM up to 4 times instead of once. This is deliberately NOT a problem
 * for network cost — `loading="lazy"` never fires for an image inside a
 * `display:none` ancestor, so only the active breakpoint's images are
 * ever actually requested; the cost is purely extra (inert) DOM nodes,
 * which is a good trade for a layout that's mathematically gap-proof
 * rather than one more heuristic that happens to work today.
 *
 * Column counts use raw px media queries at this project's actual
 * breakpoints (390/768/1024/1440, not Tailwind's stock sm/md/lg/xl —
 * see the site-wide "sm redefined to 390" lesson).
 *
 * FULL PAGE WIDTH (Cathal, July 16 2026 second pass): "exactly as
 * described from squarespace example, full page width, 8px between and
 * left and right." Deliberately NOT wrapped in the site's Container
 * primitive by its callers — fills its parent <section>. Two named
 * padding variants (`padding` prop, not a raw number — avoids fragile
 * per-breakpoint arithmetic for two very different use cases):
 *   - "tight" (default): flat 8px every breakpoint, matching the inter-
 *     image gap exactly — used by /portraits and /concerts.
 *   - "wide": 24/48px responsive (mobile/tablet+) — used by the hub's
 *     curated overview block. Matches Container's own 24/48px scale
 *     exactly — see [[PhotographyChooser]] for the same fix.
 * Don't wrap either variant in <Container> — that reintroduces the
 * 24/48px site gutter on top of this component's own padding.
 *
 * Gap: 8px both directions — real flexbox `gap` now (not a fake
 * column-gap + margin-bottom substitute).
 *
 * LIGHTBOX: delegated to PhotoLightbox.astro. Duplicate `[data-lightbox-
 * trigger]` elements (one photo now has up to 4, one per breakpoint
 * wrapper) are safe — PhotoLightbox wires a click listener to every
 * matching element via forEach, and only the currently-visible one can
 * ever receive a click, so this needed no changes.
 *
 * GROUPING (Cathal, July 21 2026): `groupSize` "closes" the flow after N
 * images — the item list is chunked into independent blocks, each
 * balancing its OWN columns only (at each breakpoint). Appending more
 * items only ever affects the newest (possibly partial) trailing group;
 * every earlier group is permanently locked once it hits `groupSize`.
 * Global `data-index` (for the shared lightbox's prev/next order) is
 * preserved across group boundaries — it's the position in the full
 * flat `items` array, not reset per group. Left undefined (default,
 * single group) for /portraits and /concerts, which want one continuous
 * flow across the whole archive.
 */
import PhotoLightbox from "./PhotoLightbox.astro";
import { distributeColumns, getAspectRatio } from "../../lib/photography";

interface Item {
  src: string;
  alt?: string;
}

interface Props {
  items: Item[];
  /** Fallback alt text for images that don't have a specific one. */
  altFallback?: string;
  /**
   * Caps the column count below the default 2/3/4/5 responsive scale —
   * used by the /work/photography hub's overview block (capped at 3,
   * July 16 2026: Cathal wants the flavour grid narrower than the full
   * collection pages, which stay at 5).
   */
  maxColumns?: number;
  /** "tight" = flat 8px (archive pages). "wide" = 24/48px responsive (curated overview). */
  padding?: "tight" | "wide";
  /**
   * Splits `items` into sealed masonry blocks of this many images each,
   * stacked vertically, instead of one continuous flow across the whole
   * array. See the file-level GROUPING note above. Omit for the default
   * single-flow behaviour.
   */
  groupSize?: number;
  /**
   * Use `maxColumns` at EVERY breakpoint instead of the default
   * responsive 2/3/5 scale-down on smaller screens (see MOBILE COLUMN
   * OVERRIDE below for why this exists).
   */
  fixedColumns?: boolean;
}

const {
  items,
  altFallback = "Photograph by Cathal Mac an Bheatha",
  maxColumns = 5,
  padding = "tight",
  groupSize,
  fixedColumns = false,
} = Astro.props;

// Every image without its own caption would otherwise get the exact same
// alt text repeated (170+ times on /portraits) — useless for screen
// reader users trying to tell images apart, and duplicate-alt spam for
// image search. Appending a position ("image 14 of 172") only to
// FALLBACK alts (never to a real caption someone wrote) gives at least
// some differentiation without asking Cathal to hand-caption the whole
// archive. Revisit if/when real per-image captions exist.
const resolveAlt = (item: Item, i: number) => item.alt ?? `${altFallback}, image ${i + 1} of ${items.length}`;
const dataForClient = items.map((item, i) => ({ src: item.src, alt: resolveAlt(item, i) }));

// Chunk into fixed-size, independently-balanced groups when `groupSize`
// is set; otherwise a single group holding everything (identical to the
// old single-flow behaviour). `index` stays the GLOBAL position in
// `items` so the lightbox's data-index ordering is unaffected by grouping.
interface IndexedItem {
  item: Item;
  index: number;
}
const indexed: IndexedItem[] = items.map((item, i) => ({ item, index: i }));
const groups: IndexedItem[][] = [];
if (groupSize && groupSize > 0) {
  for (let i = 0; i < indexed.length; i += groupSize) {
    groups.push(indexed.slice(i, i + groupSize));
  }
} else {
  groups.push(indexed);
}

// Column counts actually used at each breakpoint, clamped by maxColumns.
//
// lg RAISED FROM 4 TO 5 (Cathal, July 21 2026): checked the portfolio.
// cathalmb.com reference site directly at 1280px — it renders 5 columns
// there, not 4. This project's old "lg" tier (1024–1439px) was one step
// narrower than the reference at that width. Merging lg into the same
// 5-column count as xl (rather than restructuring the breakpoint tiers
// themselves) is the minimal fix — the four DOM tiers/media queries are
// unchanged, lg and xl just compute an identical layout now. Only
// checked 1280px specifically; if the reference site actually has a
// distinct 4-column step somewhere between 1024–1279px, this will need
// revisiting.
// MOBILE COLUMN OVERRIDE (Cathal, July 21 2026, Overview strip rebuild):
// dropping to 2 columns on mobile is right for the large archive pages
// (portraits/concerts), but is a real problem for the Overview strip's
// small groupSize={6} blocks specifically. Proved this with the ACTUAL
// aspect ratios of Overview's first group (measured off disk, all
// photos genuinely 2:3 or 3:2 per Cathal): that group is 3 portrait +
// 3 landscape photos, and squeezing exactly that mix into 2 columns has
// NO balanced solution — the best achievable 2-column split (2 photos
// in one column, 4 in the other) is already mathematically optimal,
// yet still ~500–600px uneven. This isn't something distributeColumns()
// can fix by trying harder; it's the geometry of 2 columns being too
// few for that content. The SAME exact 6 ratios split across 3 columns
// balance to within ~0.002 (effectively perfect) — confirmed by direct
// calculation, not just observation. `fixedColumns` keeps Overview at
// its already-narrower 3-column cap on every breakpoint instead of
// dropping further on mobile, which is what actually fixes it.
const BP_COLUMNS = {
  mobile: Math.min(fixedColumns ? maxColumns : 2, maxColumns),
  md: Math.min(3, maxColumns),
  lg: Math.min(5, maxColumns),
  xl: Math.min(5, maxColumns),
} as const;
type Breakpoint = keyof typeof BP_COLUMNS;
const BREAKPOINTS: Breakpoint[] = ["mobile", "md", "lg", "xl"];

// For each group, precompute the column assignment at every breakpoint.
// Weighted by each photo's real aspect ratio (getAspectRatio, read from
// disk at build time) so distributeColumns balances actual HEIGHT, not
// just item count — see that function's own comment for the full
// reasoning. `index` payloads (not the aspect ratio) are what render;
// the ratio is only used as the bin-packing weight.
//
// sortForBalance DROPPED AGAIN (Cathal, July 21 2026, same day — "I'd
// like a mix of portrait and landscape images across, rather than 3
// portrait, 3 landscape, repeat"): sorting by descending weight before
// assignment was added to fix the Overview strip's old 2-column
// imbalance, and it did balance the height — but it does that by
// clustering all portraits first, then all landscapes, in the sorted
// traversal, which then lands as "portrait on top in every column, then
// all landscapes underneath" — structurally identical for every column,
// every group. Directly computed the alternative: WITHOUT sorting (using
// Cathal's own curated OVERVIEW_ORDER sequence as-is) at the now-fixed
// 3 columns, every group balances just as well (~0.001 difference, same
// as sorted) AND produces a genuine mix — e.g. one group's three columns
// came out portrait-over-landscape, landscape-over-portrait, landscape-
// over-portrait, not one repeating pattern. So the real fix for the
// original 2-column problem was the column count (fixedColumns above),
// not the sort — order-preserving greedy was fine all along once there
// were enough columns for the content. distributeColumns() no longer
// gets a sortForBalance argument from here.
type ColumnPayload = { item: Item; index: number };
const groupLayouts = groups.map((group) => {
  const payloads: ColumnPayload[] = group.map(({ item, index }) => ({ item, index }));
  const layout = {} as Record<Breakpoint, ColumnPayload[][]>;
  for (const bp of BREAKPOINTS) {
    layout[bp] = distributeColumns(payloads, BP_COLUMNS[bp], (p) => getAspectRatio(p.item.src));
  }
  return layout;
});
---

<div class="photo-masonry-wrap" data-reveal>
  {
    groups.map((group, gi) => (
      <div class={`photo-masonry photo-masonry--${padding}`}>
        {
          BREAKPOINTS.map((bp) => (
            <div class={`pm-tier pm-bp-${bp}`}>
              {
                groupLayouts[gi][bp].map((col) => (
                  <div class="pm-col">
                    {
                      col.map(({ item, index }) => (
                        <button
                          type="button"
                          class="photo-masonry-item"
                          data-lightbox-trigger
                          data-index={index}
                          aria-label={`View fullsize: ${resolveAlt(item, index)}`}
                        >
                          <img src={item.src} alt={resolveAlt(item, index)} loading="lazy" />
                        </button>
                      ))
                    }
                  </div>
                ))
              }
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    ))
  }
</div>

<PhotoLightbox items={dataForClient} />

<style>
  .photo-masonry-wrap {
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* Gap BETWEEN groups (Cathal, July 21 2026 — "spacing issues between
       the blocks"): groups are independent masonry blocks with naturally
       ragged bottom edges (different columns end at different heights —
       expected). Without an explicit gap here they were stacking with
       zero space, so a ragged bottom edge would jam straight into the
       next group's top row. 8px matches the same rhythm used everywhere
       else in this component (column gap, inter-image gap). */
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .photo-masonry {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .photo-masonry--tight {
    padding: 0 8px;
  }

  .photo-masonry--wide {
    padding: 0 24px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .photo-masonry--wide {
      padding: 0 48px;
    }
  }

  /* Exactly one breakpoint tier is ever visible. Each is a fixed,
     always-rendered wrapper (never dynamically classed) so these rules
     never fight each other — see the file-level RESPONSIVE COLUMN
     COUNTS note above. */
  .pm-tier {
    display: none;
    gap: 8px;
    /* Without this, flex's default `align-items: stretch` force-stretches
       every column to match the tallest column in the row, leaving
       visible blank space below whatever a shorter column's own photos
       actually fill (Cathal, July 21 2026 — "gap issues every block").
       flex-start lets each column be exactly as tall as its own content
       and nothing more. */
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .pm-bp-mobile {
    display: flex;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .pm-bp-mobile {
      display: none;
    }
    .pm-bp-md {
      display: flex;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .pm-bp-md {
      display: none;
    }
    .pm-bp-lg {
      display: flex;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1440px) {
    .pm-bp-lg {
      display: none;
    }
    .pm-bp-xl {
      display: flex;
    }
  }

  .pm-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .photo-masonry-item {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    cursor: zoom-in;
  }

  .photo-masonry-item img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
